"Works backward from an undesired top event to the combinations of lower-level failures that could produce it.\n\nUse when: Analyze how equipment and human failures can combine. Inspect whether redundant protections share a common cause.\n\nAvoid when: The question is which outcomes follow an initiating event.\n\nVisual grammar: Event nodes describe definite failure conditions. AND and OR gates express different logical combinations. Basic events and transfer references distinguish modeling endpoints and reused subtrees.\n\nBuild: Define the top event and analysis boundary precisely. Deduce immediate causes and connect them with appropriate logic gates. Decompose until meaningful basic events or justified analysis limits are reached.\n\nCheck: Gate semantics match the actual required failure combination. Repeated and common-cause events are identified consistently. Probability calculations, if used, respect dependence assumptions.\n\nPitfalls: Using AND when either failure alone is sufficient. Multiplying dependent event probabilities as if they were independent.\n\nExample: A cold-storage loss tree separates power failure, cooling failure and monitoring failures, identifying combinations that defeat protective measures.\n\nAlternatives: Event tree, Bow-tie diagram."
